// Cycle of Life: Realistic Fauna — FotSA Old World deer (Cervinae).
//
// As of 0.1.14 this mod ships exactly one species: Megaloceros giganteus, the giant deer or "Irish
// elk" — the largest deer that ever lived, a 500-600 kg animal carrying 3.5 m of antler, and a
// genuine Holocene survivor (it outlasted the Pleistocene by thousands of years, which is what earns
// it a place in this mod). The file is structured for the whole subfamily, so red deer, sika,
// fallow, muntjac and the rest will presumably arrive in later versions and want adding here.
//
// The mod gets the important things right. Body weights are per-sex and accurate to the published
// estimates (female 500, male 600), so they are inherited untouched, and unlike its sibling Bovinae
// it does declare a maxQuantityByGroup. Spawn blocks are air/soil/sand/gravel/snow/grass with no
// water, so nothing needs excluding. Climate gating is already tight: -4 to 10 C, high ground,
// partly wooded, with a 1e-05 runtime chance and a 100-block player standoff. This is meant to be a
// rare animal and it is.
//
// TWO CHANGES, both toward the published biology:
//
// 1. DIET. The mod gives it Fruit/Vegetable — a woodland browser's signature. The evidence puts it
//    at the other end: dental mesowear and microwear across Middle-to-Late Pleistocene populations
//    show generalised mixed feeding tending toward GRAZING, and stable isotopes (d13C/d18O) from
//    Irish molar enamel indicate a diet of grass and forbs supplemented with browse — the Irish
//    late-glacial population being the most graze-dominated of all. So Grain (this model's grass
//    category) is added and kept first, with Vegetable for forbs and Fruit for the browse
//    supplement. That also puts it in proper competition with the other open-country grazers
//    instead of with the woodland browsers.
//
// 2. CARRYING CAPACITY. The mod's group cap of 4 reads as 4 animals per 0.173 km² — the area the
//    founding check covers — which is ~23 per km². That is red deer density for an animal three
//    times a red deer's mass; a 500-600 kg deer of open woodland belongs nearer 1-3 per km², which
//    rounds to the floor of 1. Worth saying plainly that this barely changes anything on its own: a
//    single herd grown to MaxHerdSize (14, from the mod's own group size of ~7) already exceeds any
//    K this small, so the real spacing between herds comes from the territory radius set in
//    territories.json, not from K. The number is corrected because it should read true, not because
//    it is doing heavy lifting.
//
// VERIFICATION STATUS: keys derived from the mod's own entity JSON, not read off a live roster —
// this mod is not installed on the pack author's server. A wrong key is inert (the entry is ignored
// and the animal keeps its auto-discovered values); `/col species` lists the authoritative keys.
{
  "species": {

    // Giant deer. Long-lived, single calf, slow to replace itself — and big enough, with enough
    // antler, that a wolf pack thinks twice, so it sits close to the moose on vulnerability.
    "cervinae:cervidae-cervini-megalocerosgiganteus": {
      "carryingCapacity": 1,
      "foodCategories": ["Grain", "Vegetable", "Fruit"],
      "growthRate": 0.15, "predationVulnerability": 0.45,
      "lifecycle": { "lifespanDays": 6500, "gestationDays": 250, "litterSizeMin": 1, "litterSizeMax": 1, "breedingMonths": [5], "juvenileMortality": 0.45 }
    }

  }
}
